  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on small-probability outcomes (less than 20%) and adjusts their weight upward by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, both outcomes (1000 EUR and 0 EUR) occur with 50% probability, which is not less than 20%, so no probability weighting adjustment applies. In Lottery B, the outcome of 500 EUR occurs with 100% probability, so there are no small-probability outcomes. Since no outcome in either lottery has a probability below 20%, the adjusted weighting rule does not alter any probabilities. Therefore, the reason evaluates both lotteries using their objective probabilities and compares the weighted sums. However, because the adjustment only applies to probabilities <20%, and neither lottery has such outcomes, the decision reason effectively reduces to expected value calculation in this case. The expected value of Lottery A is 500 EUR, same as Lottery B (500 EUR), so the reason would be indifferent between the two lotteries.",
